sleep and have sexual
intercourse with them.
The victims won't wake
up during sex, but may
have a dream about it.


[msg book_0x76]
{F2 08 FF FF}A male demon of popular
European folklore during
the medieval ages. They
visit women in their 
sleep and have sexual 
intercourse with them. 
The offspring resulting
from this will become 
witches and wizards.


[msg book_0x77]
{F2 08 FF FF}Fomor are Celtic giants
of evil. Many are 
hideous-looking 
creatures, like trolls.
Led by Balor, they 
fend off invaders.


[msg book_0x78]
{F2 08 FF FF}The children of Lilith.
Like their mother, they
have sex with men at 
night. Their legend 
spread to Greece where
they are known as 
the 'Empusa'.


[msg book_0x79]
{F2 08 FF FF}A giant eagle in Norse
mythology who lives at 
the top of Yggdrasil. 
One flap of his wings 
can create storms. He 
is always fighting with 
Nidhogg, the giant 
serpent at the bottom 
of the tree.


[msg book_0x7A]
{F2 08 FF FF}A mysterious, 
unidentified being 
that was spotted
in West Virginia 
around the 1960's. 
It was regarded as 
a parody of a popular 
superhero at the time.


[msg book_0x7B]
{F2 08 FF FF}Japanese mythical
creatures said to fall
to the earth along with
thunder. They usually
run through the clouds,
but come down riding on
lightning. They are said
to resemble weasels.


[msg book_0x7C]
{F2 08 FF FF}A legendary monster in
Japanese mythology.
It has the head of
a monkey, the arms
and legs of a tiger
and the tail of a snake.
It was first discovered
hidden in a black cloud
as it attacked
an emperor's palace.


[msg book_0x7D]
{F2 08 FF FF}An evil creature that
looks like a horse with
two curving horns. It is
said to be the opposite
of a Chichevache.


[msg book_0x7E]
{F2 08 FF FF}A bird in Chinese 
mythology said to 
have poison in its
feathers, because it 
eats poisonous snakes. 
Dipping a feather into 
wine will turn it into 
a deadly poison, able 
to kill anyone with 
one drop.


[msg book_0x7F]
{F2 08 FF FF}A type of Preta in Hindu
and Buddhist mythology.
Classified as 'powerful
ghosts', they come in 
different shapes and 
forms. Some are skinny,
some are fat. Some are
tall, some are short.
They can control the
dead and eat humans.


[msg book_0x80]
{F2 08 FF FF}The spirit who said,
"For we are many,"
in ancient scriptures.
The name comes from
the Roman military term
for an army unit of
3000 to 6000 men.


[msg book_0x81]
{F2 08 FF FF}Demons of disease in Sri
Lankan mythology. Masks
of them are made so that
shamans can talk with
them. They can be 
summoned and exorcised
through ritual dancing
while wearing the
mask.


[msg book_0x82]
{F2 08 FF FF}A demon that was
summoned by a sorcerer
named Crowely at the
beginning of the
20th century. When he
lost control of the
demon it possessed him.
It is the last hurdle in 
conquering the dark side
of the ego.


[msg book_0x83]
{F2 08 FF FF}They are 'hungry ghosts'
as they are unable to
swallow food. This is a
punishment in
reincarnation. One with
too many desires in
life is doomed to spend
time as a Preta.


[msg book_0x84]
{F2 08 FF FF}It can transform into
any person it sees. It 
is said that whomsoever 
meets it will die within
a few days, and much
folklore stems from this
creature. They may also
become physical 
incarnations of 
psychological ailments.


[msg book_0x85]
{F2 08 FF FF}A mass of malevolent
intent and emotion that
failed to become a true
demon. All it does is
eat prey and grow.


[msg book_0x86]
{F2 08 FF FF}An infamous jelly-like
monster. It swallows
humans and grows. It is
said that the only way
to stop blobs is to 
freeze them.


[msg book_0x87]
{F2 08 FF FF}A primitive life form
made of a jelly-like
substance. Slime have no
true origin, though they
say these creatures 
enjoy collecting shiny 
objects.


[msg book_0x88]
{F2 08 FF FF}Spirits of those who
could not ascend to
heaven in Japanese
folklore. Some have
evil intent, but
they are fairly
weak.


[msg book_0x89]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the more famous
names for the mysterious
lights seen over swamps.
They are the souls of
those who fail to go
to the afterlife and
instead wander the 
wilderness, misleading
travellers.


[msg book_0x8A]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the four major
angels. His name means,
'Who is like god?'. He
stands at the top of
the angel hierarchy.
He carries an unusually
long sword that can cut
through anything.


[msg book_0x8B]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the four major 
angels. She is also 
the only female angel 
at this rank. Her name
comes from the Sumerian 
word for 'governor'. 


[msg book_0x8C]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the four major
archangels. His name 
means 'healer'. He 
explains the history 
of the fallen angels 
and the creation of
Adam and Eve.


[msg book_0x8D]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the four major
archangels. His name 
means 'flame of god'. 
He knows all of the 
celestial phenomena. 
He is also the first 
angel Satan met
on earth.


[msg book_0x8E]
{F2 08 FF FF}The elephant-headed god
in Hindu mythology. He
was originally created
by Parvati out of dirt 
to prevent anyone from 
watching her bathe.
Shiva knocked his head
off, but replaced it
with an elephant's head.


[msg book_0x8F]
{F2 08 FF FF}'Choosers of the slain' 
in Norse mythology. 
Armed with shining armor
and spears, they look 
for the brave to take
to Valhalla, so that they
may fight in Ragnarok.


[msg book_0x90]
{F2 08 FF FF}A major deity in ancient
Japanese mythology.
Clay dolls were often
sculpted in its image.
Because this god was
worshipped by a rebel,
the dolls came to be
considered symbols
of defiance.


[msg book_0x91]
{F2 08 FF FF}A member of the Tengu 
race in Japanese 
mythology. They are
the most powerful and
popular of all Tengu.
They have the power to
fend off evil spirits
and attract good luck.


[msg book_0x92]
{F2 08 FF FF}A hero of Hindu descent.
He can transform into
anything, fly and has 
great strength. He 
performed many heroic 
deeds in the Ramayana.
He is depicted as a 
monkey.


[msg book_0x93]
{F2 08 FF FF}A legendary hero in
Celtic folklore.
His name means 'The
Hound of Culann' 
and his true name is
Setanta. There are
many tales of his
exciting adventures
with his spear
Gae Bolg.


[msg book_0x94]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the Ssu-Ling,
celestial creatures in
Chinese mythology.
It represents the
direction east, the 
season spring and 
the element wood.


[msg book_0x95]
{F2 08 FF FF}One of the Ssu-Ling,
celestial creatures 
in Chinese mythology.
It represents the
direction north, 
the season winter 
and element water.


[msg book_0x96]
{F2 08 FF FF}A mystical creature in
Balinese legend. It
represents good and is
Rangda's eternal rival.
Even if defeated, it 
will be reborn. 
The result is a 
never-ending struggle.


[msg book_0x97]
{F2 08 FF FF}A divine beast in
Japanese mythology.
They are often used to
ward off evil,
especially thefts and
fire. But, there is 
also a belief that it 
eats people. Wolves can 
become Makami.


[msg book_0x98]
{F2 08 FF FF}A divine bird-man in
Hindu mythology. He
